steps.body:
Predict the three lines this prints before you read on. Write your guesses down.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3};
    v.push_back(4);
    std::cout << v.size() << '\n';

    v.resize(2);
    std::cout << v.back() << '\n';

    std::vector<int> w(3);
    std::vector<int> u{3};
    std::cout << w.size() << ' ' << u.size() << '\n';
}
```

The last line is the one people get wrong. Parentheses and braces mean different things here.

### Why containers, and which one

A raw C array (`int a[10]`) doesn't know its own size, decays to a pointer the moment you pass it to a function, and can't grow. Modern C++ replaces it with two standard containers:

- **`std::array<T, N>`**: fixed size known at compile time. Lives wherever you declare it (on the stack for a local), no heap allocation, same speed as a C array, but it knows `size()`, can be copied and compared, and works with range-for and the algorithms. Use it for things whose size is part of the meaning: an RGB colour (`std::array<int, 3>`), a 3D point, the 4 wheel speeds of a robot.
- **`std::vector<T>`**: dynamic size. The elements live in one contiguous block on the heap; the vector object itself is three pointers (begin, end of elements, end of capacity). Use it as your default container whenever the count is known only at run time: sensor readings, students, lines of a file.

Both store elements **contiguously**, which is why they are fast: the CPU cache loads neighbours for free. That's also why `std::vector` is the right default even when you think you want a linked list.

### Construction: braces vs parentheses

`std::vector<int> w(3);` calls the "count" constructor: three value-initialized ints, `{0, 0, 0}`. `std::vector<int> u{3};` is list initialization: one element, `{3}`. So the pretest prints `4`, `2`, then `3 1`. When you mean "n copies", use parentheses: `std::vector<double> zeros(n, 0.0);`.

### Size vs capacity

`size()` is how many elements exist. `capacity()` is how many fit before the vector must reallocate. When `push_back` finds `size() == capacity()`, the vector allocates a bigger block (libstdc++ doubles it), moves every element across and frees the old block. That's why `push_back` is *amortized* O(1): most calls are cheap, a few are expensive, and the total over n pushes is O(n).

Two consequences:
1. If you know the final count, call `reserve(n)` first. One allocation instead of about log₂ n.
2. **Reallocation invalidates** every pointer, reference and iterator into the vector. Don't keep `int& first = v[0];` across a `push_back`.

`resize(n)` changes the *size* (adding default values or dropping the tail). `reserve(n)` changes only the *capacity*. Mixing them up is a classic bug: after `v.reserve(5)`, `v[0] = 1` is undefined behaviour because no element 0 exists yet.

### Access: `[]` vs `at()`

`v[i]` does no bounds check: out of range is undefined behaviour (it may "work", crash, or corrupt something else). `v.at(i)` checks and throws `std::out_of_range`. Use `at()` while learning and in code that handles untrusted indices; use `[]` in tight loops whose bounds you control. Indices are `std::size_t` (unsigned), so `for (int i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)` gives a signed/unsigned warning; prefer range-for, or `std::size_t i`.

### Passing and returning

Pass containers **by const reference** to read them (`const std::vector<int>&`), by plain reference to modify the caller's container, and **return new containers by value**. Returning by value is cheap: the compiler either constructs the result directly in the caller (copy elision) or moves it, which just steals three pointers.

### Worked example: sensor readings

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>
#include <vector>

// Read-only input: const reference. Result: returned by value.
std::vector<double> above(const std::vector<double>& xs, double limit) {
    std::vector<double> out;
    for (double x : xs)
        if (x > limit) out.push_back(x);
    return out;
}

double mean(const std::vector<double>& xs) {
    if (xs.empty()) throw std::invalid_argument("mean of empty vector");
    double sum = 0.0;
    for (double x : xs) sum += x;
    return sum / static_cast<double>(xs.size());
}

int main() {
    std::vector<double> cm;          // distance readings in cm
    cm.reserve(6);                   // we know roughly how many
    for (double r : {42.0, 38.5, 12.0, 55.1, 9.8, 40.0}) cm.push_back(r);
    std::cout << "size " << cm.size() << ", capacity " << cm.capacity() << '\n';

    std::vector<double> far = above(cm, 30.0);
    std::cout << "far readings: " << far.size() << ", mean " << mean(far) << '\n';

    std::array<int, 3> rgb{255, 128, 0};   // size is part of the type
    std::cout << "rgb has " << rgb.size() << " channels, green = " << rgb[1] << '\n';

    try {
        std::cout << cm.at(10) << '\n';
    } catch (const std::out_of_range&) {
        std::cout << "at(10) threw out_of_range\n";
    }

    std::vector<int> growth;
    std::size_t last = growth.capacity();
    for (int i = 0; i < 20; ++i) {
        growth.push_back(i);
        if (growth.capacity() != last) {
            last = growth.capacity();
            std::cout << last << ' ';
        }
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}
```

On g++ the last line prints `1 2 4 8 16 32`: each reallocation doubles the capacity. The exact growth factor is up to the library (MSVC uses 1.5), so never write code that depends on it.

### 2D data

`std::vector<std::vector<int>> grid(rows, std::vector<int>(cols, 0));` is the readable way to make a matrix. Each row is its own heap block, which is fine for now; numeric code later uses one flat `std::vector<double>` of size `rows * cols` indexed as `r * cols + c`, the same layout NumPy uses.

