`delay(1000)` stops the processor for a second. During that second it can't read the bumper, the distance sensor or the serial port. A robot driving toward a wall while it waits out a delay is the classic first crash.

**The pattern.** `millis()` returns milliseconds since power-on as an `unsigned long` (32 bits on the Uno). Instead of waiting, check every pass through `loop()` whether enough time has passed:

```arduino-snippet
unsigned long lastBlink = 0;
const unsigned long BLINK_MS = 500;

void loop() {
  unsigned long now = millis();
  if (now - lastBlink >= BLINK_MS) {
    lastBlink += BLINK_MS;          // keeps a steady rhythm; '= now' also works but drifts
    toggleLed();
  }
  // ...other tasks run here every pass, thousands of times a second
}
```

Each task gets its own "last time" variable and interval, so several run side by side: blink every 500 ms, read the ultrasonic sensor every 60 ms, print status every 200 ms, and check the button on every pass.

**Rollover.** After 2³² ms (about 49.7 days) `millis()` wraps back to 0. Written as `now - last >= interval`, the unsigned subtraction still gives the right elapsed time across the wrap. Written as `now >= last + interval`, it breaks at the wrap. You can prove this on your Mac with plain C++, using `uint32_t` for the Uno's 32-bit `unsigned long`:

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::uint32_t last = 4294967000u;   // 296 ms before the wrap
    std::uint32_t now = 200u;           // 200 ms after the wrap
    std::uint32_t interval = 400u;

    std::uint32_t elapsed = now - last; // wraps correctly: 496
    std::cout << "elapsed " << elapsed << ", due: " << (elapsed >= interval) << '\n';

    std::uint32_t deadline = last + interval;   // overflows to 104
    std::cout << "deadline-style check says due: " << (now >= deadline)
              << " (right by luck here; it fires early whenever last + interval wraps)\n";
}
```

The rule for students: **always subtract, never add**.

In **Wokwi**, new Arduino Uno project: LED on pin 13 (built in is fine), a second LED on pin 12 with a 220 Ω resistor, a pushbutton on pin 2 to GND (use `INPUT_PULLUP`).

1. **Blocking version first.** Blink the pin-13 LED with `delay(500)` twice per loop, and turn the pin-12 LED on while the button is pressed. Press the button quickly: it responds late or not at all.
2. **Non-blocking version.** Rewrite with `millis()`: one task blinks pin 13 every 500 ms, one task copies the button to pin 12 on every pass, one task prints `loops per second` every 1000 ms from a counter.

```arduino
const int LED_BLINK = 13, LED_BTN = 12, BTN = 2;
unsigned long lastBlink = 0, lastReport = 0, loops = 0;
bool blinkOn = false;

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_BLINK, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(LED_BTN, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(BTN, INPUT_PULLUP);
  Serial.begin(115200);
}

void loop() {
  unsigned long now = millis();
  loops++;

  if (now - lastBlink >= 500) {           // task 1: blink
    lastBlink += 500;
    blinkOn = !blinkOn;
    digitalWrite(LED_BLINK, blinkOn);
  }

  digitalWrite(LED_BTN, digitalRead(BTN) == LOW);   // task 2: every pass

  if (now - lastReport >= 1000) {         // task 3: report
    lastReport += 1000;
    Serial.print("loops per second: ");
    Serial.println(loops);
    loops = 0;
  }
}
```

3. Add a fourth task of your own: a second LED blinking every 130 ms. Confirm both rhythms stay steady together.

