Predict the output. One line contains a subtle bug: find it before running anything.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v{4, 1, 3, 1, 5};
    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());
    auto it = std::find(v.begin(), v.end(), 3);
    std::cout << (it - v.begin()) << '\n';
    std::cout << std::count(v.begin(), v.end(), 1) << '\n';
    std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 1);
    std::cout << v.size() << '\n';
}
```

### Iterators: positions, not indices

An iterator is a generalized position in a container. For a vector it behaves like a pointer: `*it` is the element, `++it` moves to the next one, `it - v.begin()` is the index. For a `std::map` it's a node in the tree, and `++it` walks to the next key in order. The point of the abstraction is that **one algorithm works on every container** that can hand out iterators.

Every range is **half-open**: `[begin, end)`. `begin()` points at the first element, `end()` points *one past the last*, never at an element, so you never dereference it. Half-open ranges make the empty range trivial (`begin == end`), make the size `end - begin`, and make "not found" easy to express: algorithms return `end` when they find nothing. That's the same convention as Python's slices and your binary search's `hi = len(nums)`.

### The everyday algorithms

All in `<algorithm>`, except `accumulate` in `<numeric>`:

| Call | Does | Cost |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `std::sort(b, e)` / `std::sort(b, e, cmp)` | sort in place (not stable) | O(n log n) |
| `std::stable_sort` | sort, equal elements keep their order | O(n log n) |
| `std::find(b, e, x)` | iterator to first `x`, or `e` | O(n) |
| `std::find_if(b, e, pred)` | first element where `pred` is true | O(n) |
| `std::count(b, e, x)`, `std::count_if(b, e, pred)` | how many | O(n) |
| `std::accumulate(b, e, init)` | sum (type of `init` decides the arithmetic!) | O(n) |
| `std::min_element`, `std::max_element` | iterator to the extreme | O(n) |
| `std::lower_bound(b, e, x)` | first element `>= x` in a **sorted** range | O(log n) |
| `std::binary_search(b, e, x)` | bool, sorted range | O(log n) |

`lower_bound` *is* the binary search you're writing by hand this week in the algorithms block. Once you've written it yourself, use the library one.

A trap with `accumulate`: `std::accumulate(v.begin(), v.end(), 0)` on a `vector<double>` sums into an `int` and truncates every step. Write `0.0`.

### Lambdas

A C++ lambda is an inline function object: `[capture](params) { body }`. The capture list says which local variables it can see: `[limit]` copies `limit` in, `[&total]` refers to `total`, `[]` captures nothing. Unlike Python's, the body can be any number of statements.

```cpp-snippet
int limit = 30;
auto far = [limit](double d) { return d > limit; };
```

### Removing elements: the trap in the pretest

`std::remove` can't change a container's size: it only gets iterators, and an iterator can't erase from its container. It shifts the kept elements forward and returns an iterator to the new logical end; the tail holds leftovers. So the pretest prints `2` (3 sits at index 2 after sorting to 1 1 3 4 5), `2`, and then `5`: the vector is still size 5. The old fix is the **erase-remove idiom**, `v.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 1), v.end());`. In C++20 just write `std::erase(v, 1)` or `std::erase_if(v, pred)`.

### Worked example: ranking students

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <numeric>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct Student {
    std::string name;
    int done;      // lessons completed
    int total;     // lessons assigned
    double rate() const { return total ? static_cast<double>(done) / total : 0.0; }
};

int main() {
    std::vector<Student> s{{"Aru", 18, 20}, {"Bek", 9, 20}, {"Dana", 18, 20}, {"Erlan", 14, 20}};

    // Sort by completion, best first; ties broken by name so the order is deterministic.
    std::sort(s.begin(), s.end(), [](const Student& a, const Student& b) {
        if (a.done != b.done) return a.done > b.done;
        return a.name < b.name;
    });
    for (const auto& st : s) std::cout << st.name << ' ' << st.done << '\n';

    // How many are behind (under 75%)?
    auto behind = std::count_if(s.begin(), s.end(), [](const Student& st) { return st.rate() < 0.75; });
    std::cout << "behind: " << behind << '\n';

    // Total lessons done: init 0 (int) is right because done is int.
    int total_done = std::accumulate(s.begin(), s.end(), 0,
                                     [](int acc, const Student& st) { return acc + st.done; });
    std::cout << "total done: " << total_done << '\n';

    // First student under 50%, if any.
    auto it = std::find_if(s.begin(), s.end(), [](const Student& st) { return st.rate() < 0.5; });
    if (it != s.end()) std::cout << "needs help: " << it->name << '\n';

    // Binary search on a sorted vector of scores.
    std::vector<int> scores{55, 61, 70, 70, 84, 92};
    auto lb = std::lower_bound(scores.begin(), scores.end(), 70);
    std::cout << "first 70 at index " << (lb - scores.begin()) << '\n';

    // C++20 removal: drop everyone under 50%.
    std::erase_if(s, [](const Student& st) { return st.rate() < 0.5; });
    std::cout << "remaining: " << s.size() << '\n';
}
```

Output: `Aru 18`, `Dana 18`, `Erlan 14`, `Bek 9`, then `behind: 2` (Erlan at 70%, Bek at 45%), `total done: 59`, `needs help: Bek`, `first 70 at index 2`, `remaining: 3`.

### The comparator rule

A sort comparator must be a **strict weak ordering**: `cmp(a, a)` is false, and it behaves consistently. Writing `<=` instead of `<` breaks this, and `std::sort` may then read out of bounds. If you ever see a crash inside `std::sort`, check the comparator first.

C++20 also has `std::ranges::sort(v)` and friends, which take the container directly. They're nicer to read; learn the iterator versions first because every codebase (including ROS 2) is full of them.

